    "topic": "people used to sleep in two shifts",
    "summary": "Historian A. Roger Ekirch argues that before the Industrial Revolution, interrupted sleep was dominant in the West: a first sleep, about an hour awake around midnight ('the watch') used to pray, reflect, visit neighbours, have sex or commit petty crime, then a second sleep. He suggests electric lighting ended it. In Thomas Wehr's 1992 study, seven men given fourteen hours of darkness daily for a month slept about four hours, woke for two to three, then slept four more.",
    "angle": "Waking in the night may just be sleeping the old way.",
